          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Herodotus as \"The Father of History\"</strong><br>\nHerodotus is often called the \"Father of History\" because he was the first to collect and systematically document events and present them in a narrative form. He aimed to explain the causes of events, such as wars and great disasters, and to provide evidence for the stories he related. This systematic approach was new to Greek literature at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Methodology of Herodotus</strong><br>\nHerodotus often reported various versions of the stories he heard, sometimes expressing his own opinion on their likelihood. He placed great emphasis on direct observation and firsthand accounts, even when they contained supernatural elements. His approach highlights the importance of inquiry and skepticism, hallmarks of the historical method.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticisms of Herodotus's Work</strong><br>\nDespite his innovative methods, Herodotus faced criticism for including tales that seemed far-fetched or biased. Some ancient and modern scholars have accused him of making up stories or being too credulous of his sources. However, these criticisms often reflect the challenge of discerning fact from fiction in historical narratives.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
