          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Herodotus's Influence on Later Historians</strong><br>\nHerodotus influenced countless historians after him, including Thucydides, who also wrote about the Greek world but took a more analytical and less narrative approach. However, Thucydides acknowledged the foundation that Herodotus laid for the study of history and continued the practice of rigorous source evaluation and chronicling events.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Legacy of \"The Histories\"</strong><br>\nThe Histories has had a lasting impact on both history and literature. It not only informed future generations about Greek and Persian cultures but also influenced the way stories and histories were told. Its narrative technique and structure have been admired and imitated by writers for centuries following its publication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Herodotus's Work in Modern Times</strong><br>\nToday, Herodotus's work is still important for the study of antiquity and serves as a primary source on the Persian Wars and the customs of the historical period he describes. His work continues to be a subject of scholarly debate, influencing modern understandings of history writing and the ancient world. His storytelling technique, combining research with entertainment, endures in the way history is taught and appreciated.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
